   "text": "A home Wi-Fi network carries a video stream from the internet to a laptop through a series of steps involving a router, access point, radio channels, and packet transmission. Here's a simplified explanation:\n\n1. **Router and Access Point**: The router is the central hub of your Wi-Fi network. It connects to the internet and broadcasts a wireless signal that other devices can receive. The access point is a type of router that is specifically designed to provide a stronger signal in a larger area, like a home.\n\n2. **Radio Channels**: Wi-Fi operates on radio frequencies, which are divided into channels. Each channel can carry a different signal. The router and access point use these channels to transmit data. The channels are divided into 20 MHz segments, and there are 13 channels available in most regions.\n\n3. **Packet Transmission**: When you send a video stream, it is broken down into smaller units called packets. Each packet contains a small part of the video data. The router and access point use the radio channels to transmit these packets. The packets are sent in a sequence, and the router and access point work together to ensure that the packets are delivered in the correct order.\n\n4. **Transmission Over Multiple Channels**: If you have multiple devices transmitting at once"
